azgor wrote:
> [1]: Nurses should sometimes carry stethoscopes. --lament

A 1% probability would be about right.

> [2]: Spellcasters (the Wizard class) should gain a bonus to all spells
> (be it a bonus to damage, bonus to healing, etc..) when casting while
> not wielding a weapon. This bonus should increase by +1 every 5 XL.
> --aristotle73

If the idea is that the nimble finger'd make better spellcasters, then
maybe instead: grant a reduction in failure rate for high level spells
(5, 6, 7) when wielding nothing, and a further reduction for wearing no
gloves (except GoD). Also, raise failure rate for greased fingers or
wearing cursed rings.

> [3]: In reference to Ideas[2], perhaps several different classes should
> each have bonuses to a single spell category; e.g. priests to clerical,
> healers to healing, rogues to escape, etc. --PsyMar

The current system of per-class special spells provides more
opportunity for class differentiation. It does make sense that Healers
should be good at *all* healing spells though...

> [4]: Elven weapons enchantable to +9. Why does only armor get the
> bonus? --jmr

There may be a balance issue: a stack of +7 elven daggers is already
pretty good.

> [5]: A greased ring should slide off your finger (unless the ring is
> cursed). --froggey

The curse is powerful enough to resist you tugging it off, so it's
probably powerful enough to resist a little grease.

> [6]: It should be possible to bribe some angry @ (eg. guards, priests)
> with money, with a luck penalty to lawfuls. --Pthag

An alignment penalty rather than a Luck penalty, surely?

> [7]: NH balrogs explode cataclysmicly when they die, like D&D balors
> --GreyKnight

NetHack has surely borrowed enough from D&D.

> [11]: Quantum mechanics could carry a single random potion on them, or
> possibly a writing utensil (marker). --Ian-Keith

More generally, I'd like to see the death-drop system overhauled. The
monster should be generated with the death-droppable item in their
inventory, and they should be able to use it. The item should also be
appropriate for the monster's size.

> [12]: Force bolting a huge chunk of meat should result in a bunch of
> meatballs --PsyMar

I think force bolts are a kind of magical cricket bat: you can smash or
hurt brittle or soft things, but whacking a huge chunk of meat isn't
going to do anything.

> [13]: There should be a limit to how much can be written on the ground.
>  "There's not enough space to write here." when attmepting to add a
> message that would surpass this limit to an engraving. --aristotle73
> [how about "...to write that here." -az]

Squares of dungeon floor seem big enough to hold dragons.

> [15]: Koschei the Deathless, a named arch-lich --lament

I gave up playing SLASH'EM when I found it featured mind flayers named
after the spice girls.

